**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Introduction to Composites and Laminated Composite Plates; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 3.4–3.6 and 9.2, extending to the relevant material-specific literature through their references.

A machine contains more than structural metals. Elastomers seal and isolate; polymers carry loads and slide; ceramics supply wear, temperature or electrical functions; composites can place stiffness along selected directions. Their behavior may be strongly dependent on orientation, moisture, temperature, rate, interfaces and processing defects.

For ideal aligned continuous fibres under equal axial strain, a simple stiffness estimate is E_parallel = V_f E_f + (1−V_f)E_m. An equal-stress serial idealization instead adds compliances. Neither expression is a general prediction for every transverse or woven composite. Interfacial load transfer, fibre waviness, porosity and damage require their own evidence.

With V_f = 0.6, E_f = 200 GPa and E_m = 3 GPa, the ideal axial estimate is 121.2 GPa. Strength, toughness and fatigue do not follow from that modulus average. A brittle specimen's failure distribution can depend on sampled stressed volume and flaws; a metal's yield criterion is not an automatic substitute.

**Work:** Compare at least three material families for one mechanical function, retaining thermal, environmental, manufacturing and repair constraints. For a laminate, transform a ply stiffness into common axes and examine bending–extension coupling. Give evidence for a failure mode that a scalar equivalent-stress plot would miss.

